+F2 improved failure ranking over sensor statistics, while its advantage over equal
+averaging remained unresolved. The saved-score audit identifies beneficial within-fold
+ordering changes from the visual term; the absent matched sensor-only second-stage
+control still limits attribution of the full pooled gain. The original gates showed no
+general advantage. Known absence returned the surviving branch's prediction; useful
+responses to corruption remained a separate, unresolved problem.
+
+The favorable follow-ups changed different parts of the pipeline. Longer PatchTST
+training partly closed the gap to sensor statistics without eliminating it; combining
+the two representations improved AP within the same sensing modality. Last-quarter
+pooling used the end of the completed action. Anchored adaptation improved ranking over
+its anchor and constant-adjustment control, but reduced recall and increased calibration
+error relative to the anchor. The spatial-encoder and fine-tuning comparisons showed
+that added processing or updated weights need not improve fusion; inconclusive tuning
+intervals do not establish equivalence. The augmentation-matched and quality-interaction
+comparisons left their primary robustness contrasts unresolved.
+
+No run combined boundary pooling, the same-sensor hybrid and anchored adaptation.
+Choosing these components after comparing their results on the same recordings could
+compound selection bias: the choices may reflect that cohort's particular
+characteristics. A joint pipeline would need to be fixed before evaluation on untouched
+recordings. Natural faults and shifts, independently adjudicated labels, new seeds,
+predictions before action completion and uncertainty beyond the fixed fits remain
+open.\ev{Controlled follow-ups and uncertainty}{\EvidenceRef{E038}{},
